      {
        "review_id": 268457652,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r268457652-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Interesting but no info",
        "text": "My first inrtoducation to the temple area was a fence with signs posted on stating Wold Heritage Site, through the fence howver was open area, covered in mounds of litter with cows, donkeys and people digging around. \n\nThe temple area is full of interesting sights. Unfortunately, if you are not a muslim you cannot get into it but have to watch from the outside, the best view is across the river on the raised area nearby. \n\nThe Sadhu are grat photography subjects, and they know it, and they will charge for it. R50 is the normal going rate although some will demand R100. Make sure you secure the rate BEFORE taking photos. \n\nThere is a wonderful old age home on the right, before the temple and river river area. Go inside and meet the the folk there, they are lonely, without families and super friendly. It is also heart breaking. \n\nIf you are sensitive you should avoid looking out over the river as the locals celebrate and prepare their dead for  burning on the river banks, they then walk them over to the otherside of the bridge to the burning platforms where they light them and watch them burn, before sweeping up the ashes into the \"holy river\". \n\nThe smell is not as unpleasant as expected when you think they are burning bodies, but they are well prepared and covered in some herbs and spices, but if you are sensitive it, you may wish to avoid this part.\n\nThe local monkeys move freely across the whole area, occasionally flinging themselves from rooftops into the \"holy river\" to cool dowm. It act of childlike play enjoyed by both the monkeys and all how watch. \n\nThere are plenty of local ladies who will - for a donation - bless you, paint you and pray over you.  This is fun and unless you are very religious (in which case you may feel uncomfortable partaking in some other religious routine) makes feel you welcome.\n\nThe temple area is strange collection of differnt buildings and it is worth exploring them all, however unless you have a guide to explain, there is no or little info there. \n\nI enjoyed my time at the temple. \n\n",
