      {
        "review_id": 267505535,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r267505535-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Soulful and sacred place -- a must-see",
        "text": "Out of all the temples I've visited in Nepal, I found this to be the most inspiring and with such ceremonial significance.  Boudanath Stupa looks more impressive, and Swayambhunath is a lot of fun, but there's nothing quite like Pashupatinath. \n\nI was fortunate to have my good friend Naba (a local and an excellent guide to boot) show me around the temple complex and explain to me in great detail what each statue, each area and each ceremony meant.  During my visit there, I was able to also witness a cremation ceremony, which is powerfully moving. Since majority of Hindu Nepalis are cremated in Pashupatinath, there's something quite awe-inspiring about witnessing the end in a sacred place like this.\n\nI would highly recommend getting a guide or a local to take you around, as the grounds are wide and there is much detail to look at. Wear comfortable shoes as you may end up climbing up and down some very huge steps. For a bit of fun and cultural experience, have your photo taken with the saddhus (holy men) for a small token fee (they like accepting currencies from different countries, by the way!).  I also got to talk to them a bit, which was quite nice.\n\nThere is a small cafe near the top of this temple complex where they serve really good milk tea. For foreign travelers like me, entrance fee to Pashupatinath is Nepalese Rs 1,000 (US $10) which seemed pretty steep to me at first, but was actually worth it, in the end.",
        "review_tip": null,
